        {
          "id": "domain-7-topological",
          "title": "Order dependency work only when the graph is acyclic",
          "concept_ids": [
            "dsa.graph.topological",
            "dsa.graph.cycle_state"
          ],
          "prerequisite_node_ids": [],
          "map_position": {
            "x": 0.12,
            "y": 0.5
          },
          "prompts": [
            {
              "id": "what",
              "kind": "what",
              "label": "WHAT IS A TOPOLOGICAL ORDER?",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "For a directed acyclic graph, a topological order places every prerequisite before each dependent vertex. If a directed cycle exists, no such complete ordering is possible.",
                "progressive_reveal": {
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "id": "before",
                      "text": "prerequisite before each dependent",
                      "required": true
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "cycle",
                      "text": "If a directed cycle exists",
                      "required": true
                    }
                  ]
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "real-use",
              "kind": "connects_to",
              "label": "REAL USE: BUILD AND DEPLOYMENT DEPENDENCIES",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "A build pipeline can model 'library must build before service' or 'database migration must finish before app deploy' as directed edges. Topological ordering yields a valid execution order; cycle detection exposes impossible dependency plans.",
                "progressive_reveal": {
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "id": "build",
                      "text": "valid execution order",
                      "required": true
                    }
                  ]
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "mental",
              "kind": "mental_model",
              "label": "PREREQUISITES, NOT PRIORITIES",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Topological order satisfies dependency constraints but is not necessarily unique and does not mean globally 'best' priority. Several valid orders may exist when independent work can be scheduled in either order.",
                "progressive_reveal": {
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "id": "notunique",
                      "text": "not necessarily unique",
                      "required": true
                    }
                  ]
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "title": "Princeton Algorithms — Directed Graphs",
              "url": "https://algs4.cs.princeton.edu/42digraph/"
            },
            {
              "title": "MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006 — Depth-First Search",
              "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-006-introduction-to-algorithms-spring-2020/pages/lecture-notes/"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "domain-7-union-find",
          "title": "Maintain connected components as edges arrive",
          "concept_ids": [
            "dsa.graph.union_find"
          ],
          "prerequisite_node_ids": [
            "domain-7-topological"
          ],
          "map_position": {
            "x": 0.44,
            "y": 0.5
          },
          "prompts": [
            {
              "id": "what",
              "kind": "what",
              "label": "WHEN IS UNION-FIND THE RIGHT SHAPE?",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Disjoint-set union is useful when the main operations are: add a connection between two entities, and ask whether two entities currently belong to the same connected component.",
                "progressive_reveal": {
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "id": "operations",
                      "text": "add a connection",
                      "required": true
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "same",
                      "text": "same connected component",
                      "required": true
                    }
                  ]
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "real-use",
              "kind": "connects_to",
              "label": "REAL USE: MERGE NETWORK SEGMENTS AS LINKS COME ONLINE",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "If physical or logical links are activated over time, union-find can merge the components containing each link endpoint. Later connectivity queries can ask whether two devices are already connected through the activated links.",
                "progressive_reveal": {
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "id": "links",
                      "text": "activated over time",
                      "required": true
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "queries",
                      "text": "already connected",
                      "required": true
                    }
                  ]
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "not-this",
              "kind": "not_this",
              "label": "UNION-FIND DOES NOT GIVE YOU THE PATH",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": false,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "It answers component membership efficiently, but it does not directly return a shortest path or the sequence of edges between two vertices. Use traversal when path structure is required.",
                "progressive_reveal": {
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "id": "notpath",
                      "text": "does not directly return a shortest path",
                      "required": true
                    }
                  ]
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "source_refs": [
